From 590d049db8ccc0e012d23b26483360b87c5d422d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AuraLite <auralite@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 19:43:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Upkeep U3: gdbstub publishes and accepts the real x87 state

Upkeep U0 measured that target.xml reserves registers 24-39
for the x87 stack and control block, but reg_hex emitted
zeros for every ST, FCW alone for the control group and a
hard-wired all-empty FTAG, while reg_set silently ignored
all of them ("no x87 data model" -- stale since FPU F1 gave
the CPU a full physical register file, tags, TOP and
last-instruction pointers).

The block is live now, under a convention measured at both
ends before a byte was exposed.  GDB's i387-tdep.c
(i387_print_float_info) iterates PHYSICAL registers, takes
tag = (ftag >> 2*physical) & 3 and reads the value from
regcache slot (physical + 8 - TOP) % 8; therefore st_i is
the LOGICAL register (physical (TOP+i)&7) and ftag is the
full 16-bit tag word keyed by physical register.  QEMU's
target/i386/gdbstub.c read side rotates the same way and
re-derives FSTAT's TOP from fpstt; both are mirrored.
fiseg/fioff/foseg/fooff/fop publish the CPU's recorded
fields (QEMU returns 0; this CPU tracks them and tells the
truth; the 32-bit XML slots truncate FIP/FDP).

Two measured QEMU behaviours are deliberately not copied:
its write side indexes fpregs[] physically (no rotation,
so set $st0 does not name the register p $st0 read) and
never updates tags.  Here an ST write lands in the logical
register's slot through cpu_x87_st_from80 (x87_w padding
normalization + x87_class re-tagging: FRSTOR-admissible
state only), and an FSW write moves ftop with TOP through
cpu_x87_set_fsw -- four small bridge functions are cpu.c's
new export surface; its converters stay private.  On a
host whose long double is not the 80-bit format the ST
registers answer 'x' (RSP unavailable) and writes no-op --
the FPU #UDs there already, and no bytes are fabricated.

Nothing else on the wire moves: the g packet is still
592 bytes, and protocol lanes A-C pass unmodified.

Vectors: lane D of tests/test_gdbstub.py (27 new checks,
57 total) stops an FPU ROM payload (fninit/fld1/fldpi/
fldl2e/fldz, TOP=4) and pins the rotated stack
byte-for-byte, ftag 0x01FF, fstat 0x2000, FIP/FCS/FOP
naming the FLDZ; P writes -2.5/0.5/a denormal into
st0/st3/empty-st7 (tags 0x01FF -> 0x00BF) plus a new FCW;
resumes into four FSTP m80 stores and reads them back from
guest memory; an FSW write then moves TOP under the stop.
Real-client half: GNU gdb 16.3 was vendored onto this host
for the phase, so test-gdb runs lane G for real and new
lane F checks gdb's own info float rendering (R4 Zero,
TOP: 4, Tag Word 0x01ff, FIP f000:ff08, Opcode 0xd9ee),
set $st0/$st3 and the FSTP results in memory; both lanes
SKIP-clean where gdb is absent.  Lane F drives by stepi:
measured on the wire, the stub's breakpoints are linear
while gdb reconciles a swbreak hit against raw $pc, so a
real-mode ROM breakpoint auto-continues as a spurious trap
(flat modes are unaffected) -- recorded, not U3's to fix.

NC: the new protocol lane on the pre-U3 tree (b6d048b)
fails exactly 16 lane-D checks (zeros, tag 0xFFFF, ignored
writes) with lanes A-C green.  Gates: make test (incl.
test-gdb with real gdb) and make test-sanitize green;
both SeaBIOS ISO lanes green for real.
